# C# Standards

Follow [Microsoft's C# coding conventions](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/fundamentals/coding-style/coding-conventions) in general.

**Lint**: Run Visual Studio **Code Cleanup** with these fixers enabled:
- Format document
- Remove unnecessary imports/usings
- Sort imports/usings
- Apply file header preferences

## File encoding

- Use UTF-8 **without BOM** for all C# files.
- `using` directives must not start with a byte-order mark (no `﻿using`).

## Error handling

- Avoid empty `catch { }` blocks. Either log, rethrow, or handle meaningfully.
- When intentionally swallowing exceptions (e.g. defensive reflection where failure is acceptable), add a one-line comment so reviewers know it is intentional.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD – silent swallow
try { candidate = p.GetValue(value); } catch { }

// ✅ GOOD – intentional swallow documented
try { candidate = p.GetValue(value); } catch { } // Reflection; ignore missing/inaccessible member
```

## Harmony patches

- Patch classes: `internal static`, `[HarmonyPatch(typeof(TargetType))]`.
- Use `AccessTools` / `HarmonyLib` for reflection when possible.
- Keep patch logic focused; move non-trivial logic into shared helpers or services.

## Namespaces and usings

- One namespace per file matching folder structure (e.g. `WaypointQueue.Patches` for `Patches/*.cs`).
- Sort `using` directives: external (System, HarmonyLib, etc.) then project namespaces (WaypointQueue.*).
- Remove unnecessary usings; use Code Cleanup "Sort usings" and "Remove unnecessary usings" before committing.
